(195424, 10875, 1570, '<td valign="top">I''d like to thank everybody for their comments... just one explanation - I didn''t edit this picture at all, that''s the answer to questions like "what did you do with it" and comments about the "grain"... as far as the comments about darkness and gloom - that''s exactly the feeling I was going for... of course, I would love it if all the comments were like the one from Becky, but I try to learn something from every one of them... thanks again!<br/> <br/>PS: the suggestion about neatimage proved to be very important, i''ve been using it even since!<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2008-10-19 11:11:49.</i></td>', 'I''d like to thank everybody for their comments... just one explanation - I didn''t edit this picture at all, that''s the answer to questions like "what did you do with it" and comments about the "grain"... as far as the comments about darkness and gloom - that''s exactly the feeling I was going for... of course, I would love it if all the comments were like the one from Becky, but I try to learn something from every one of them... thanks again! PS: the suggestion about neatimage proved to be very important, i''ve been using it even since!', '2003-01-20 10:08:12.000000', '2008-10-19 12:11:49.000000', 0),
(195438, 11011, 2170, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB!<br/> <br/>Congratulations on taking a great shot overall.<br/> <br/>Here''s what I like:<br/>The focus and clarity are both excellent.  The subject is nicely composed and very well shot.<br/> <br/>Here''s what I think could be better:<br/>Too much foreground -- the bird is too close to the top of the screen.  The horizon in the background also seems to cut him in half.  Shooting from a lower angle to put the hawk against a background of the sky would make him stand out even more.<br/> <br/>Because this is a "landscape" challenge, something just makes me think that this would be better in "landscape" mode -- meaning, keep the bird where he is in the frame, but give us a long view in the direction he is looking.  I''m really frustrated because I found a great article about this on the web somewhere but am having trouble finding it now.  DANG!  If I find the link, I''ll post it later.  Anyway, you get the point...what''s the bird seeing?  Why is he so still?  Or is he just staring off into space?  We should see that in any case, but especially to help this photo fit into the challenge more.<br/> <br/>Last but not least, the golden tint to the photo is overwhelming.  If you have a software application that will allow you to adjust the color balance (or hue and saturation), play around with those to see what a difference it can make.  If you don''t have access to Photoshop or something like it, try THE GIMP, which is a free alternative and is supposed to be pretty good.<br/> <br/>Hope this helps....<br/> <br/>Congrats on a good score and a great capture.</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB! Congratulations on taking a great shot overall. Here''s what I like:The focus and clarity are both excellent.  The subject is nicely composed and very well shot. Here''s what I think could be better:Too much foreground -- the bird is too close to the top of the screen.  The horizon in the background also seems to cut him in half.  Shooting from a lower angle to put the hawk against a background of the sky would make him stand out even more. Because this is a "landscape" challenge, something just makes me think that this would be better in "landscape" mode -- meaning, keep the bird where he is in the frame, but give us a long view in the direction he is looking.  I''m really frustrated because I found a great article about this on the web somewhere but am having trouble finding it now.  DANG!  If I find the link, I''ll post it later.  Anyway, you get the point...what''s the bird seeing?  Why is he so still?  Or is he just staring off into space?  We should see that in any case, but especially to help this photo fit into the challenge more. Last but not least, the golden tint to the photo is overwhelming.  If you have a software application that will allow you to adjust the color balance (or hue and saturation), play around with those to see what a difference it can make.  If you don''t have access to Photoshop or something like it, try THE GIMP, which is a free alternative and is supposed to be pretty good. Hope this helps.... Congrats on a good score and a great capture.', '2003-01-20 10:13:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(195481, 10981, 1202, '<td valign="top">For those of you that may be interested we had pretty poor weather in central CA during this challenge week.   It was very foggy in the valley where I live so I headed for some higher elevation.   The hills of the coast range are always so dry in the summer and fall months I decided to go there to capture some of the green from recent rain.   This picture was taken just south of the entrance to the Morgan Territory Regional Reserve, just east of the Oakland bay area.   I waited quite awhile for the haze to lift but It only started getting thicker so this was the best I could get on this trip.<br/>Thanks to all of you that enjoyed this picture, it has been a pleasure to share some of the west coast landscape with you.<br/>Dick<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2003-01-20 16:30:26.</i></td>', 'For those of you that may be interested we had pretty poor weather in central CA during this challenge week.   It was very foggy in the valley where I live so I headed for some higher elevation.   The hills of the coast range are always so dry in the summer and fall months I decided to go there to capture some of the green from recent rain.   This picture was taken just south of the entrance to the Morgan Territory Regional Reserve, just east of the Oakland bay area.   I waited quite awhile for the haze to lift but It only started getting thicker so this was the best I could get on this trip.Thanks to all of you that enjoyed this picture, it has been a pleasure to share some of the west coast landscape with you.Dick', '2003-01-20 10:27:29.000000', '2003-01-20 16:30:26.000000', 0),
