(195671, 11028, 772, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :)<br/> <br/>Hi Sulamk!  I received your email and I feel lucky to have gotten your photo to critique.  This will be an example of the types of critiques we are looking for in the Critique Club...<br/> <br/>I love a waterfall.  I enjoy photographing them myself and here are a few examples of some I have done in the past:<br/> <br/><a href="//www.pbase.com/image/5520374" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">//www.pbase.com/image/5520374</a> <br/><a href="//www.pbase.com/image/5555604" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">//www.pbase.com/image/5555604</a> <br/><a href="//www.pbase.com/image/8563364" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">//www.pbase.com/image/8563364</a> <br/> <br/>I think you did a great job of capturing this particular waterall in the context of the ''landscape'' challenge.  Your vista in this particular shot covers the waterfall and plenty of surrounding land as well :)  <br/> <br/>One of the things about this image that I particularly like is the color contrasts.  The blue sky / red rocks / greenery work very well together in this image.  The waterfall itself is just a nice single element that makes this image stand out.  I believe that all great landscape shots, contrary to the finishing order in the challenge this week, need some single ''strong'' element for the viewer''s eye to come to rest on.  Your waterfall profides that to me in this image.  <br/> <br/>As for a possible improvement on this image...<br/> <br/>I''m looking at your camera settings.  My personal preference on waterfall photos, as shown in my examples, is ''soft'' water created by a slower shutter speed with the camera.  There is a simple trick to getting this.  Your aperture was set to F4.5 and a shutter speed of 1/160".  Without knowing any better, I would assume that this was a ''point and shoot'' shot taken with the default camera settings.<br/> <br/>If you had chosed aperture priority and setup with a smaller aperture (larger F number), your shutter speed would have slowed down some which would have created ''softer'' water in this shot.  You can also get even longer shutter speeds in a situation like this by using a polarizer or a neutral density filter on your camera.  <br/> <br/>For more information about shutter and aperture priority photo modes, take a look at my tutorial on DPC at:<br/> <br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/tutorial.php?TUTORIAL_ID=3" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">Controlling Your Exposure</a> <br/> <br/>This tutorial explains how aperture and shutter priority affect your images...<br/> <br/>Keep up the good work and I will be in touch with some links for you on critiques very soon :)<br/> <br/>John Setzler<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :) Hi Sulamk!  I received your email and I feel lucky to have gotten your photo to critique.  This will be an example of the types of critiques we are looking for in the Critique Club... I love a waterfall.  I enjoy photographing them myself and here are a few examples of some I have done in the past: //www.pbase.com/image/5520374 //www.pbase.com/image/5555604 //www.pbase.com/image/8563364  I think you did a great job of capturing this particular waterall in the context of the ''landscape'' challenge.  Your vista in this particular shot covers the waterfall and plenty of surrounding land as well :)   One of the things about this image that I particularly like is the color contrasts.  The blue sky / red rocks / greenery work very well together in this image.  The waterfall itself is just a nice single element that makes this image stand out.  I believe that all great landscape shots, contrary to the finishing order in the challenge this week, need some single ''strong'' element for the viewer''s eye to come to rest on.  Your waterfall profides that to me in this image.   As for a possible improvement on this image... I''m looking at your camera settings.  My personal preference on waterfall photos, as shown in my examples, is ''soft'' water created by a slower shutter speed with the camera.  There is a simple trick to getting this.  Your aperture was set to F4.5 and a shutter speed of 1/160".  Without knowing any better, I would assume that this was a ''point and shoot'' shot taken with the default camera settings. If you had chosed aperture priority and setup with a smaller aperture (larger F number), your shutter speed would have slowed down some which would have created ''softer'' water in this shot.  You can also get even longer shutter speeds in a situation like this by using a polarizer or a neutral density filter on your camera.   For more information about shutter and aperture priority photo modes, take a look at my tutorial on DPC at: Controlling Your Exposure  This tutorial explains how aperture and shutter priority affect your images... Keep up the good work and I will be in touch with some links for you on critiques very soon :) John Setzler', '2003-01-20 12:03:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(195672, 10851, 1280, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Comment by Grayce M. Dillon aka Gracious<br/> <br/>I love the composition.  Everything is placed just right.  The color of the sky is a definite plus.  <br/>One of my favorite aspects is the wonderful lighting on the windmill.<br/>I like that you didn''t crop out the vegetation in the right corner. First it adds dimension, and also echoes the color of the mill.<br/>The diagonal lines work quite well and add appeal, especially the fence leading to the mill.<br/>Several textures in here at work together too. eg: ice, vegetation, windmill, water.<br/>Good use of opposites...eg warm sky, cold landscape.  <br/>OVer all a very pleasing image.  Good work!</td>', 'Critique Club Comment by Grayce M. Dillon aka Gracious I love the composition.  Everything is placed just right.  The color of the sky is a definite plus.  One of my favorite aspects is the wonderful lighting on the windmill.I like that you didn''t crop out the vegetation in the right corner. First it adds dimension, and also echoes the color of the mill.The diagonal lines work quite well and add appeal, especially the fence leading to the mill.Several textures in here at work together too. eg: ice, vegetation, windmill, water.Good use of opposites...eg warm sky, cold landscape.  OVer all a very pleasing image.  Good work!', '2003-01-20 12:05:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(195680, 10739, 1280, '<td valign="top">Looks like a delightful road.  I can almost feel the coast dowwwwwwwwwwwn....weeeeeeeeeeeeeee.<br/>All the greenery is very appealing.  Darkened up a bit would bring those greens out even more.  <br/>The perspective is really good.  Like how the road veers off to (our) right.<br/>Focus could be a little sharper.<br/>Nice image.</td>', 'Looks like a delightful road.  I can almost feel the coast dowwwwwwwwwwwn....weeeeeeeeeeeeeee.All the greenery is very appealing.  Darkened up a bit would bring those greens out even more.  The perspective is really good.  Like how the road veers off to (our) right.Focus could be a little sharper.Nice image.', '2003-01-20 12:13:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(195688, 10977, 1280, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Comment by Grayce M. Dillon aka Gracious<br/> <br/>I like this very much.  Very moody...cold, isolation, barren.  Being bright causes the contrast of the tree to be silhouetted...very nice.  <br/>The composition could be improved with a simple change of perspective.  The barn being centered is not the most appealing. But I do like the way the branches frame the barn on one side.<br/>You show several dimensions here...foreground grasses, tree, field, barn, more grasses, horizon, sky.  Nice.</td>', 'Critique Club Comment by Grayce M. Dillon aka Gracious I like this very much.  Very moody...cold, isolation, barren.  Being bright causes the contrast of the tree to be silhouetted...very nice.  The composition could be improved with a simple change of perspective.  The barn being centered is not the most appealing. But I do like the way the branches frame the barn on one side.You show several dimensions here...foreground grasses, tree, field, barn, more grasses, horizon, sky.  Nice.', '2003-01-20 12:20:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(195695, 10928, 1280, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Comment by Grayce M. Dillon aka Gracious<br/> <br/>The color of the sky is phenominal and contrasts beautifully with the warm-colored landscape. The single tree on the horizon is nicely framed by the foreground branches, which also adds depth.<br/>having the whole image so centered makes it a little stagnant and predictable, but overall it is a lovely image and I like it.<br/>I''d reconsider using a blue border.  Black or white border would be better. <br/></td>', 'Critique Club Comment by Grayce M. Dillon aka Gracious The color of the sky is phenominal and contrasts beautifully with the warm-colored landscape. The single tree on the horizon is nicely framed by the foreground branches, which also adds depth.having the whole image so centered makes it a little stagnant and predictable, but overall it is a lovely image and I like it.I''d reconsider using a blue border.  Black or white border would be better. ', '2003-01-20 12:26:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(195728, 10716, 2221, '<td valign="top">Should have placed better in my opinion. I was one of the two tens ;-)</td>', 'Should have placed better in my opinion. I was one of the two tens ;-)', '2003-01-20 13:07:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(195751, 10977, 2221, '<td valign="top">Thank you for your comments. Actually I''m pleased with the score. It''s an average photo and personally I thik that the tree in the foreground could be a bit sharper. I rotated the image a little bit in postprocessing and I thing that made it a bit blurry overall.<br/> <br/>I don''t really agree about the centered barn. What I liked about the photo are the three different "layers" depth-wise. From left to right the tree, barn and horizon. So to me it made sense to crop the photo so that the tree fills the left side, the barn is centered and the little trees in the background on the right side.<br/> <br/>hbunch7187: Yes, your eyes were playing tricks on you ;-) The photo is just black &amp; white.<br/> <br/>Thanks again,<br/>Until next week.</td>', 'Thank you for your comments. Actually I''m pleased with the score. It''s an average photo and personally I thik that the tree in the foreground could be a bit sharper. I rotated the image a little bit in postprocessing and I thing that made it a bit blurry overall. I don''t really agree about the centered barn. What I liked about the photo are the three different "layers" depth-wise. From left to right the tree, barn and horizon. So to me it made sense to crop the photo so that the tree fills the left side, the barn is centered and the little trees in the background on the right side. hbunch7187: Yes, your eyes were playing tricks on you ;-) The photo is just black & white. Thanks again,Until next week.', '2003-01-20 13:34:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(195772, 10827, 5223, '<td valign="top">I love the sky and colour well done [one thing i do not understand  you were given  a vote of 1  and three 2 for shuch a great photo]</td>', 'I love the sky and colour well done [one thing i do not understand  you were given  a vote of 1  and three 2 for shuch a great photo]', '2003-01-20 13:42:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(195838, 11007, 472, '<td valign="top">EXCELLENT FINISH, Dave.</td>', 'EXCELLENT FINISH, Dave.', '2003-01-20 14:22:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(195876, 10925, 1143, '<td valign="top">Well... That''s quite a snow for Texas! This photo doesn''t really have any glaring problems, but it isn''t as strong as many of the other submissions. It IS rather understated and serene which doesn''t help it stand out from the crowd. I really like the sidewalk, but it just isn''t very obvious in B&amp;W. I actually think that you could move a little, showing more of the sidewalk, and make it a stronger element in the image. I''m still not sure that I like this in black and white. Did you take it in color and then convert it? B&amp;W can often add drama to a landscape, but IMO it makes this photo seem a little too lifeless and blah. Since you obviously have ready access to this site, I would suggest going back in spring, summer (maybe even during a rain shower), and fall to take the same shot from the same spot. Putting them side by side and showing how much it changes would be very interesting.</td>', 'Well... That''s quite a snow for Texas! This photo doesn''t really have any glaring problems, but it isn''t as strong as many of the other submissions. It IS rather understated and serene which doesn''t help it stand out from the crowd. I really like the sidewalk, but it just isn''t very obvious in B&W. I actually think that you could move a little, showing more of the sidewalk, and make it a stronger element in the image. I''m still not sure that I like this in black and white. Did you take it in color and then convert it? B&W can often add drama to a landscape, but IMO it makes this photo seem a little too lifeless and blah. Since you obviously have ready access to this site, I would suggest going back in spring, summer (maybe even during a rain shower), and fall to take the same shot from the same spot. Putting them side by side and showing how much it changes would be very interesting.', '2003-01-20 14:47:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(195990, 10948, 4852, '<td valign="top">I am suprised this didnt do much better.It obviously didnt appeal to the tastes of the general group of viewers.  The votes are so spread out , and the amount of low scores is near the amount of highest scores, mine being one of the 10''s. Those that voted you down have no artistic sense about them and are loser''s !   Great Job !</td>', 'I am suprised this didnt do much better.It obviously didnt appeal to the tastes of the general group of viewers.  The votes are so spread out , and the amount of low scores is near the amount of highest scores, mine being one of the 10''s. Those that voted you down have no artistic sense about them and are loser''s !   Great Job !', '2003-01-20 15:51:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(196038, 10838, 4494, '<td valign="top">Congrats!  I thought this one might win. :)  Good job!</td>', 'Congrats!  I thought this one might win. :)  Good job!', '2003-01-20 16:23:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(196043, 11033, 4494, '<td valign="top">I really love this picture so I''ll make another comment...  What really "makes it" for me is the level of the camera being right at the height of the rails.  Don''t know why, but I really like that perspective for this one!</td>', 'I really love this picture so I''ll make another comment...  What really "makes it" for me is the level of the camera being right at the height of the rails.  Don''t know why, but I really like that perspective for this one!', '2003-01-20 16:29:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(196054, 10995, 714, '<td valign="top">Joann, just to let you know.  I didn''t vote this a 1.  I voted it a 6.  I do like it.  Great shot.  I would have perfered less water.  You did pretty good.</td>', 'Joann, just to let you know.  I didn''t vote this a 1.  I voted it a 6.  I do like it.  Great shot.  I would have perfered less water.  You did pretty good.', '2003-01-20 16:38:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(196066, 10959, 3032, '<td valign="top"><b>~~~~Critique Club Comment~~~~<br/> <br/>Composition (content)</b> <br/>Nice abstract image. An original approach on reflections from water. The result of the ripples distorting the already curved tree and tree branches is very nice.<br/>The composition is very interesting. There is a kind of double antithesis in it (right vs left). You''ve got two fat trees on the right and two thinner ones on the left. The fat ones carry a lot of branches, where the thinner ones don''t and leave room for a refreshing blue sky. The right side say''s old and the left side young (young trees reaching for the bright blue sky, where the old ones try to prevent the growing of young ones by taking away the light).<br/>Could also be cause by your position in relation to the trees and they might all be equally old, but the result remains the same.<br/> <br/><b>Background</b> <br/>Good use of reflection.<br/> <br/><b>Camera Work (Technical)</b> <br/>Good choice of aperture, the scene is a subject as a whole, so it need to be in the field of focus as a whole.<br/>Good choice of shutter to avoid motion blur by the moving water.<br/>Good exposure, reflections sometimes fools the meter.<br/>Good focus and sharpness.<br/> <br/>(Don''t have a clue if you have control over those with an S30)<br/> <br/><b>Digital Processing (technical)</b> <br/>No comments.<br/> <br/><b>My opinion</b> <br/>Original. Nice idea.</td>', '~~~~Critique Club Comment~~~~ Composition (content) Nice abstract image. An original approach on reflections from water. The result of the ripples distorting the already curved tree and tree branches is very nice.The composition is very interesting. There is a kind of double antithesis in it (right vs left). You''ve got two fat trees on the right and two thinner ones on the left. The fat ones carry a lot of branches, where the thinner ones don''t and leave room for a refreshing blue sky. The right side say''s old and the left side young (young trees reaching for the bright blue sky, where the old ones try to prevent the growing of young ones by taking away the light).Could also be cause by your position in relation to the trees and they might all be equally old, but the result remains the same. Background Good use of reflection. Camera Work (Technical) Good choice of aperture, the scene is a subject as a whole, so it need to be in the field of focus as a whole.Good choice of shutter to avoid motion blur by the moving water.Good exposure, reflections sometimes fools the meter.Good focus and sharpness. (Don''t have a clue if you have control over those with an S30) Digital Processing (technical) No comments. My opinion Original. Nice idea.', '2003-01-20 16:51:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(196069, 10897, 772, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :)<br/> <br/>Hi SJG :)<br/> <br/>The ''fog'' in this scene is definitely a nice element of the photo.  I also like the silhouetted trees framing the right side of the image.  What I don''t like about this image is the flat color tone.  Looking at the camera settings here, it looks to be deliberately underexposed, which created this effect.  I"m not sure why... maybe that was necessary to highlight the fog clouds that you reference in the title.  <br/> <br/>The only suggestion I could maybe offer to improve this shot would be to slow down the shutter and let some more exposure brighten things up a bit :)<br/> <br/>Keep up the good work :)<br/> <br/>John Setzler<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :) Hi SJG :) The ''fog'' in this scene is definitely a nice element of the photo.  I also like the silhouetted trees framing the right side of the image.  What I don''t like about this image is the flat color tone.  Looking at the camera settings here, it looks to be deliberately underexposed, which created this effect.  I"m not sure why... maybe that was necessary to highlight the fog clouds that you reference in the title.   The only suggestion I could maybe offer to improve this shot would be to slow down the shutter and let some more exposure brighten things up a bit :) Keep up the good work :) John Setzler', '2003-01-20 16:53:01.000000', NULL, 0);
INSERT INTO comments ("id", "image_id", "commenter_id", "raw_comment", "comment", "date", "edited", "made_during_challenge") VALUES
(196088, 11033, 527, '<td valign="top">That''s what made it for me too, Chris. The other photos I took that showed much more of the tracks placed too much emphasis on the tracks and began to compete with the interesting background. At least for this challenge topic. The image was about how the tracks and bridge complimented and supported the background.<br/> <br/>T</td>', 'That''s what made it for me too, Chris. The other photos I took that showed much more of the tracks placed too much emphasis on the tracks and began to compete with the interesting background. At least for this challenge topic. The image was about how the tracks and bridge complimented and supported the background. T', '2003-01-20 17:05:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(196218, 10808, 933, '<td valign="top">Excellent photo Arnit!  I am blown away this did not finish higher.  I love this photo, funky composition and all.  Great job, keep it up!!</td>', 'Excellent photo Arnit!  I am blown away this did not finish higher.  I love this photo, funky composition and all.  Great job, keep it up!!', '2003-01-20 18:14:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(196330, 10814, 6025, '<td valign="top"><b>(- Critique Club -)</b> <br/> <br/>This is a very strong image indeed. The inverse diagonals of the light/dark under the tree and the clouds on the opposite side draw your image to the right of the image. <br/> <br/>But.. uh.. there is nothing there.  It might seem ironic somehow, but my eyes are actually pulled away from the tree. I think this might be the reason for why the image fails a little bit in my eyes; In an image that has such incredibly strong visual pull towards the right, you sould almost wait to find something in the intersection of shadow and light. Anything would have done. Somebody sitting with their back to the camera (a nude? A naked back, for example? An old wooden chest, perhaps? A pot of gold? Anything, really).<br/> <br/>Technically the image is very good, but it could easily have been sharpened several degrees; I want to see the individual branchlets of that tree! (if you don''t know how to sharpen an image, <br/> <br/><a href="//www.deviantart.com/tutorial/930983" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">have a look at this tutorial</a>). Furthermore, a tad more contrast would also have been an option, although the clouds are already burnt out (I know you have a tremendous span in this image - bit I still believe your image is a tiny tad overexposed)<br/> <br/>When all that bickering is done; That is a damn fine image you have there, my friend! There is a good reason for why I gave this thing an 8. <br/> <br/>Haje Jan (SharQ)</td>', '(- Critique Club -)  This is a very strong image indeed. The inverse diagonals of the light/dark under the tree and the clouds on the opposite side draw your image to the right of the image.  But.. uh.. there is nothing there.  It might seem ironic somehow, but my eyes are actually pulled away from the tree. I think this might be the reason for why the image fails a little bit in my eyes; In an image that has such incredibly strong visual pull towards the right, you sould almost wait to find something in the intersection of shadow and light. Anything would have done. Somebody sitting with their back to the camera (a nude? A naked back, for example? An old wooden chest, perhaps? A pot of gold? Anything, really). Technically the image is very good, but it could easily have been sharpened several degrees; I want to see the individual branchlets of that tree! (if you don''t know how to sharpen an image,  have a look at this tutorial). Furthermore, a tad more contrast would also have been an option, although the clouds are already burnt out (I know you have a tremendous span in this image - bit I still believe your image is a tiny tad overexposed) When all that bickering is done; That is a damn fine image you have there, my friend! There is a good reason for why I gave this thing an 8.  Haje Jan (SharQ)', '2003-01-20 19:17:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(196361, 10959, 3320, '<td valign="top">Sander .. thank you very much for your input on my photot .. this is the first one I get from the critic club ... and it''s very interesting?<br/>thank you very much<br/>Lionel</td>', 'Sander .. thank you very much for your input on my photot .. this is the first one I get from the critic club ... and it''s very interesting?thank you very muchLionel', '2003-01-20 19:28:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(196450, 10975, 5573, '<td valign="top">Should have been top three, if not number one! Keep up the great photos. I am looking forward to seeing more from you.<br/> <br/>Todd</td>', 'Should have been top three, if not number one! Keep up the great photos. I am looking forward to seeing more from you. Todd', '2003-01-20 20:24:49.000000', NULL, 0),
(196467, 10864, 3936, '<td valign="top">Review Club Critique<br/> <br/>Very interesting photo, to say the least.  <br/> <br/>I think the coloring really makes this image.  <br/> <br/>The focus does seem a bit soft, but not fatal.  The trees are not as well defined as they could be.  The lights in the hot spot to the right center of the image are blown out, and are the only real downside to anotherwise excellent image.  Had you been able to move your location such that the hotspot was shielded from view, this could really help.  I think that simple cropping alone wouldn''t do it as you would lose a lot of the bright sky above those lights.  Perhaps if you could have chosen an angle where they were hidden by trees/bushes/rocks etc.  <br/> <br/>The hillside to the left is brighter than the surrounding area, though there is not much to be done about that.  Were it more orange like the rest of the picture, it would be better.  But that would require editing that is illegal for the challenge.  About the only other thing you could do is to play around with the overall exposure to see if that could help.  However, when it is that cold you only want to spend so long taking pictures.<br/> <br/>-alex</td>', 'Review Club Critique Very interesting photo, to say the least.   I think the coloring really makes this image.   The focus does seem a bit soft, but not fatal.  The trees are not as well defined as they could be.  The lights in the hot spot to the right center of the image are blown out, and are the only real downside to anotherwise excellent image.  Had you been able to move your location such that the hotspot was shielded from view, this could really help.  I think that simple cropping alone wouldn''t do it as you would lose a lot of the bright sky above those lights.  Perhaps if you could have chosen an angle where they were hidden by trees/bushes/rocks etc.   The hillside to the left is brighter than the surrounding area, though there is not much to be done about that.  Were it more orange like the rest of the picture, it would be better.  But that would require editing that is illegal for the challenge.  About the only other thing you could do is to play around with the overall exposure to see if that could help.  However, when it is that cold you only want to spend so long taking pictures. -alex', '2003-01-20 20:42:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(196482, 10919, 327, '<td valign="top">joshua_2415<br/><b>CC: visit</b> <br/>Hello on a Monday.<br/>Looking at your shot a Storms a Coming, I think I''m first struck by the stark and bleak look. I like that and it''s makes good photography.<br/>I would like to suggest that in this type of shot you either add more of the upper left branches or leave them out totally. They really don''t add to the shot and they do cause distraction to the eye. The lower trees I like.<br/>The title is implying a storm. The contrast in the clouds doesn''t.<br/>The focus is a little soft but that may be post processing.<br/>Not sure I''ve really added any jewel but I hope you find the critique process helpful to you in the future. Thanks for the opportunity to review your photo. Keep clicking.</td>', 'joshua_2415CC: visit Hello on a Monday.Looking at your shot a Storms a Coming, I think I''m first struck by the stark and bleak look. I like that and it''s makes good photography.I would like to suggest that in this type of shot you either add more of the upper left branches or leave them out totally. They really don''t add to the shot and they do cause distraction to the eye. The lower trees I like.The title is implying a storm. The contrast in the clouds doesn''t.The focus is a little soft but that may be post processing.Not sure I''ve really added any jewel but I hope you find the critique process helpful to you in the future. Thanks for the opportunity to review your photo. Keep clicking.', '2003-01-20 20:52:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(196491, 11018, 772, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :)<br/> <br/>Hi Kathleen :)<br/> <br/>This is a beautiful shot.  The pathway leading into the landscape is a very strong eye element in this image.  Your Canon does a beautiful job with the exposure also.  I would love to know what kind of lens you are using here also :)  I can''t really see much room for improvement on this image at all.  It looks like you probably used a polarizer on this shot, which was a good choice.  The sky seems to be a little deeper blue in the upper right.  That could be caused by two different things.  1 - the sun could have been coming in from the left, or 2 - your polarizer could have been twisted slightly the wrong way.  Turning a circular poliarzer changes the polarization... If you are using a linear polarizer, it could have been slightly off correct orientation... <br/> <br/>Another possibility with this shot would have been to get the camera slightly closer to the road here and shoot at a little higher angle also.. that can create a really nice effect in a scene like this :)<br/> <br/>Keep up the great work :)<br/> <br/>John Setzler<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :) Hi Kathleen :) This is a beautiful shot.  The pathway leading into the landscape is a very strong eye element in this image.  Your Canon does a beautiful job with the exposure also.  I would love to know what kind of lens you are using here also :)  I can''t really see much room for improvement on this image at all.  It looks like you probably used a polarizer on this shot, which was a good choice.  The sky seems to be a little deeper blue in the upper right.  That could be caused by two different things.  1 - the sun could have been coming in from the left, or 2 - your polarizer could have been twisted slightly the wrong way.  Turning a circular poliarzer changes the polarization... If you are using a linear polarizer, it could have been slightly off correct orientation...  Another possibility with this shot would have been to get the camera slightly closer to the road here and shoot at a little higher angle also.. that can create a really nice effect in a scene like this :) Keep up the great work :) John Setzler', '2003-01-20 21:03:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(196523, 10799, 5982, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by janfries:</b></div><hr/><i>Good choice of light and color.  Nice composition.</i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thanks for your comment!!</td>', 'Originally posted by janfries:Good choice of light and color.  Nice composition.  Thanks for your comment!!', '2003-01-20 21:27:27.000000', NULL, 0),
