(196995, 10981, 656, '<td valign="top">CC:  Great framing, good composition too.  I love how the hills roll to the horizon.  One thing maybe missing and common to a lot of great landscape shots is some more foreground interest.  This would add to the scale and provide maybe something to rest your eye on.  A small bush, and interesting rock and flower bravely facing the elements.  With this in the frame, you could include more foreground, less sky ?  <br/> <br/>Another option if you are looking for a clearer shot is to consider using a polariser, which would reduce some of the atmospheric haze - although personally I like the depth it has added to your picture.</td>', 'CC:  Great framing, good composition too.  I love how the hills roll to the horizon.  One thing maybe missing and common to a lot of great landscape shots is some more foreground interest.  This would add to the scale and provide maybe something to rest your eye on.  A small bush, and interesting rock and flower bravely facing the elements.  With this in the frame, you could include more foreground, less sky ?   Another option if you are looking for a clearer shot is to consider using a polariser, which would reduce some of the atmospheric haze - although personally I like the depth it has added to your picture.', '2003-01-21 12:44:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(197014, 10849, 6025, '<td valign="top"><b>(- critique club -)</b> <br/> <br/>This is a good image, but it is slightly too ''normal'' to my likings.  The sun is centered in the image, which isn''t particularly interesting. The reason behind this is the ideas of The Divine Proportions (<a href="//www.blc.edu/fac/rbuelow/FM/links.htm" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">Read this, and look for the Golden Mean and Euclid</a>) - basically, if something is placed on one of the lines that would be visible if you folded the image in three, the viewer will find the image more pleasing.<br/> <br/>Apart from the composition, you might want to reconnsider your sharpness. It looks as though you have focussed on the water close to you - remember to set your camera to infinity - but the trees in the horizon are also out of sync. You might be able to sharpen your picture a bit using Unsharp Mask (<a href="//www.photocritic.org/?page=articles&amp;id=39" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">read this article if you are not sure what USM is</a>).<br/> <br/>On the other hand, I would like to commend you for your vision. The water, the sun, the horizon and - not least - the clouds really work together to make this image worth watching.<br/> <br/>Good work, mijn vriend!<br/> <br/>Haje</td>', '(- critique club -)  This is a good image, but it is slightly too ''normal'' to my likings.  The sun is centered in the image, which isn''t particularly interesting. The reason behind this is the ideas of The Divine Proportions (Read this, and look for the Golden Mean and Euclid) - basically, if something is placed on one of the lines that would be visible if you folded the image in three, the viewer will find the image more pleasing. Apart from the composition, you might want to reconnsider your sharpness. It looks as though you have focussed on the water close to you - remember to set your camera to infinity - but the trees in the horizon are also out of sync. You might be able to sharpen your picture a bit using Unsharp Mask (read this article if you are not sure what USM is). On the other hand, I would like to commend you for your vision. The water, the sun, the horizon and - not least - the clouds really work together to make this image worth watching. Good work, mijn vriend! Haje', '2003-01-21 13:06:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(197016, 10773, 2, '<td valign="top">I''m very surprised this didn''t do better.  It probably lacks the ''wow'' factor people might be looking for after voting on 150 landscape submissions, but this photograph really has character.  I think it would also look good with the levels pulled up to very bright, bold colors, but your choice of effect is also very effective.  Great job.</td>', 'I''m very surprised this didn''t do better.  It probably lacks the ''wow'' factor people might be looking for after voting on 150 landscape submissions, but this photograph really has character.  I think it would also look good with the levels pulled up to very bright, bold colors, but your choice of effect is also very effective.  Great job.', '2003-01-21 13:09:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(197022, 11026, 2003, '<td valign="top">i agree, CQ --- hey, too bad about the disqualification of the 2nd place entry --- but now i''m #58!!! only 57 more disqualifications to go!<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2003-01-21 16:12:03.</i></td>', 'i agree, CQ --- hey, too bad about the disqualification of the 2nd place entry --- but now i''m #58!!! only 57 more disqualifications to go!', '2003-01-21 13:27:39.000000', '2003-01-21 16:12:03.000000', 0),
(197033, 10906, 4493, '<td valign="top">CC comment:<br/> <br/>Cityscape, landscape... they''re the same to me. So I like this shot. The use of a long exposure with the correct time of day has given this otherwise bland cityscape a nice cool clean look. The river leads you right into the center of the city. Do to restrictions it would have been hard to get a better angle with the river, but being dead center isn''t distracting enough to me the viewer. What impresses me the most is that with that color of blue right next to the dark lines of the buildings, it is easy to get ghosting or artifacts. I don''t see this at all. Your choice of a 10 second exposure correctly exposed this shot so that non of your lights are to bright, and the dark areas are lit well enough to make out the subtle details in the branches of the trees on the right. Only two things I can think of that would really set this picture out from the crowd... 1) If you could get your aperture smaller to have everything in tight focus, and 2) if you could elevate your perspective so that your camera is in a building or roof across from the skyline. Still retain the amount of river you see, but look more straight on at the buildings instead of up at them. This will help reduce the amount of tilt we see on the outer most building. Nice picture, very well done.</td>', 'CC comment: Cityscape, landscape... they''re the same to me. So I like this shot. The use of a long exposure with the correct time of day has given this otherwise bland cityscape a nice cool clean look. The river leads you right into the center of the city. Do to restrictions it would have been hard to get a better angle with the river, but being dead center isn''t distracting enough to me the viewer. What impresses me the most is that with that color of blue right next to the dark lines of the buildings, it is easy to get ghosting or artifacts. I don''t see this at all. Your choice of a 10 second exposure correctly exposed this shot so that non of your lights are to bright, and the dark areas are lit well enough to make out the subtle details in the branches of the trees on the right. Only two things I can think of that would really set this picture out from the crowd... 1) If you could get your aperture smaller to have everything in tight focus, and 2) if you could elevate your perspective so that your camera is in a building or roof across from the skyline. Still retain the amount of river you see, but look more straight on at the buildings instead of up at them. This will help reduce the amount of tilt we see on the outer most building. Nice picture, very well done.', '2003-01-21 13:59:35.000000', NULL, 0),
(197048, 10966, 6064, '<td valign="top">I can`t believe that some people gave 1,2,3s<br/> <br/>A great picture 9</td>', 'I can`t believe that some people gave 1,2,3s A great picture 9', '2003-01-21 14:39:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(197050, 10850, 327, '<td valign="top"><b>CC:</b> <br/> <br/>Fits The Challenge:Yes, IMHO<br/> <br/>Composition-Content:Just beautiful. The silhouettes of the trees against the sky/clouds is very nicely arranged. Over all focus is nice yet not perfect. I could do without the black frame, but it''s not obtrusive to the shot. I would of liked a different crop, maybe a 480x640 would of done this more justice.<br/> <br/>Background-Haunting cloud formation, soft lovely color. <br/> <br/>Digital Processing-Good since I don''t see it. I do think the area of sun is a over exposed but it''s small and not _that_ fried in this case. I did note the shutter speed and the ISO. <br/> <br/>My Opinion-This shot is appealing to me because of the change in light. It''s only after I''ve looked at too long and with a critical eye that I find the tree on the right a distraction. In fact I start to resent it for interrupting my view of the clouds. The clouds are this shot, the main focal point. I see where it was called a ''cloudscape''. Yes, I''d agree. I do however think the tree silhouette is stunning for the fact that it is a silhouette and we don''t see a great deal of detail. Room for improvement ......sure. Just too minor to mention. <br/>Thanks for the chance to review your landscape shot.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2003-01-21 14:42:19.</i></td>', 'CC:  Fits The Challenge:Yes, IMHO Composition-Content:Just beautiful. The silhouettes of the trees against the sky/clouds is very nicely arranged. Over all focus is nice yet not perfect. I could do without the black frame, but it''s not obtrusive to the shot. I would of liked a different crop, maybe a 480x640 would of done this more justice. Background-Haunting cloud formation, soft lovely color.  Digital Processing-Good since I don''t see it. I do think the area of sun is a over exposed but it''s small and not _that_ fried in this case. I did note the shutter speed and the ISO.  My Opinion-This shot is appealing to me because of the change in light. It''s only after I''ve looked at too long and with a critical eye that I find the tree on the right a distraction. In fact I start to resent it for interrupting my view of the clouds. The clouds are this shot, the main focal point. I see where it was called a ''cloudscape''. Yes, I''d agree. I do however think the tree silhouette is stunning for the fact that it is a silhouette and we don''t see a great deal of detail. Room for improvement ......sure. Just too minor to mention. Thanks for the chance to review your landscape shot.', '2003-01-21 14:40:55.000000', '2003-01-21 14:42:19.000000', 0),
